---
import FeaturesLayout from '../../layouts/FeaturesLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const howToJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: 'Generate Angular documentation with compodocx',
  description:
    'Three steps to produce a static documentation site for an Angular project: install the package, point it at your tsconfig, run the CLI.',
  totalTime: 'PT3M',
  supply: [
    { '@type': 'HowToSupply', name: 'Node.js 22 or newer' },
    { '@type': 'HowToSupply', name: 'An Angular 21+ project with standalone code and a tsconfig' },
  ],
  tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: '@cngxjs/compodocx' }],
  step: [
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: 1,
      name: 'Install compodocx as a dev dependency',
      text: 'Run npm install --save-dev @cngxjs/compodocx in your project root.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: 2,
      name: 'Author a doc-specific tsconfig',
      text: 'Create tsconfig.doc.json that extends your base tsconfig and excludes test, spec, and storybook files so they stay out of the rendered API surface.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: 3,
      name: 'Run the CLI',
      text: 'Run npx compodocx -p tsconfig.doc.json. Static documentation is written to ./documentation, ready to deploy.',
    },
  ],
};
---

<FeaturesLayout
  currentSlug="getting-started"
  title="Getting Started - Install compodocx & generate Angular docs"
  description="Install compodocx, point it at a tsconfig, run the CLI. Three steps to a static docs site for Angular 21+ standalone projects. Requires Node 22+."
  extraJsonLd={howToJsonLd}
>
  <p class="text-lg">
    A working compodocx setup is three things: <strong>the package</strong>,
    <strong>a tsconfig</strong> that points at your
    <a
      href="https://angular.dev"
      rel="noopener"
      target="_blank"
      class="text-cdx-primary underline underline-offset-2"
      >Angular<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a
    > source, and <strong>an output location</strong>. Defaults handle the rest. Requires
    <a
      href="https://nodejs.org/"
      rel="noopener"
      target="_blank"
      class="text-cdx-primary underline underline-offset-2"
      >Node<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a
    > 22 or newer and a project on Angular 21 or 22 written with standalone APIs.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    code={`npm install --save-dev @cngxjs/compodocx
npx compodocx -p tsconfig.doc.json`}
  />

  <p>
    The default output goes to <code class="cdx-code">./documentation/&lt;versionLabel&gt;/</code
    >.<br />
    Multi-version layout is on by default. <code class="cdx-code">versionLabel</code> is auto-detected
    from the nearest <code class="cdx-code">package.json</code>. <br />Pass
    <code class="cdx-code">--no-multiVersion</code> for the flat single-version layout.
  </p>

  <p>
    Inside an Angular workspace, <code class="cdx-code">ng add @cngxjs/compodocx</code> does the setup
    in one pass: it seeds <code class="cdx-code">tsconfig.doc.json</code> and registers
    <code class="cdx-code">compodocx:build</code>, <code class="cdx-code"
      >compodocx:build-and-serve</code
    >, and <code class="cdx-code">compodocx:serve</code> scripts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Add <code class="cdx-code">--watch --serve</code> while authoring to get a local dev server that rebuilds
    on every source change.
  </p>

  <h2>A minimal tsconfig</h2>
  <p>
    compodocx uses TypeScript&rsquo;s own resolver. A doc-specific tsconfig keeps test files,
    storybook, and dev-only entries out of the rendered API surface.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="json"
    filename="tsconfig.doc.json"
    code={`{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["**/*.spec.ts", "**/*.stories.ts"]
}`}
  />

  <h2>Coming from compodoc</h2>
  <p>
    compodocx is a fork of compodoc for modern Angular, not a drop-in replacement. These are not
    documented, and a run reports them in one legacy notice with their locations:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>NgModules and everything tied to them.</li>
    <li>
      Decorator members: <code class="cdx-code">@Input</code>, <code class="cdx-code">@Output</code
      >,
      <code class="cdx-code">@HostBinding</code>, and <code class="cdx-code">@HostListener</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Constructor injection in components, directives, pipes, and injectables.</li>
    <li>Class-based guards, interceptors, and resolvers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Switching means changing the package and the script, running it once, and acting on the warnings
    it prints. <code class="cdx-code">ng add @cngxjs/compodocx</code> removes
    <code class="cdx-code">@compodoc/compodoc</code> and rewrites your scripts. Removed flags and config
    keys are ignored with a warning instead of failing the build.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few things need work of your own. Page URLs changed (<code class="cdx-code"
      >redirects.json</code
    > maps the old paths), output is multi-version by default (<code class="cdx-code"
      >--no-multiVersion</code
    > keeps a flat folder), tools that read <code class="cdx-code">documentation.json</code> need the
    new schema, and Handlebars templates must be rewritten as JS overrides by hand.
    <code class="cdx-code">compodocx migrate jsdoc &lt;dir&gt;</code> removes retired
    <code class="cdx-code">@category</code> and <code class="cdx-code">@docsKind</code> tags. The
    <a
      href="https://github.com/cngxjs/compodocx/blob/develop/MIGRATION.md"
      rel="noopener"
      target="_blank"
      class="text-cdx-primary underline underline-offset-2"
      >migration guide<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a
    > explains each warning.
  </p>
</FeaturesLayout>
